\f0\fs40 \cf2 Nasolacrimal surgery is performed when the normal tear drainage system is blocked. When tears cannot drain properly into the nose, the eye may water constantly and there may be discharge, irritation, or repeated infections of the tear sac.\
\
The most common operation for a blocked nasolacrimal duct in adults is called dacryocystorhinostomy. This procedure creates a new drainage passage between the tear sac and the inside of the nose, bypassing the blockage. It can be performed through a small skin incision or through the nose, depending on the situation and the surgeon\'92s technique. In some cases, a small temporary tube is left in place while healing occurs.\
\
The procedure is usually intended to reduce watering and lower the chance of recurrent infection, but it may not make tearing disappear completely in every patient. Some soreness, swelling, bruising, bloody nasal discharge, or temporary watering can occur during healing.\
\
After surgery, patients are often asked to avoid nose blowing for a period of time and may be given drops, nasal spray, or antibiotics depending on the case. Follow-up visits are important, especially if a stent or tube has been placed.\
\
Risks can include bleeding, infection, scarring, failure of the new passage to remain open, persistent tearing, or the need for further treatment. Even so, this surgery is commonly performed and is often very successful in improving tear drainage and reducing symptoms.}